🔹 Scenario 1 – High GC & Latency

Problem:

Spring Boot application slows down.

Frequent Full GCs (Garbage Collections) occur.

CPU usage spikes and request latency increases.

Why it happens:

JVM spends too much time reclaiming memory because heap is full or memory is fragmented.

Application holds onto large objects or creates memory too fast.

Approach:

Check GC logs → Understand frequency, duration, and type of GC.

Analyze heap → Identify which objects are consuming memory.

Tune JVM GC parameters → Adjust heap sizes, GC algorithm, and thresholds.

Optimize memory-heavy code → Reduce object creation, use efficient data structures.

Validate with load testing → Ensure changes improve performance under real workload.

Takeaway:

Frequent full GC indicates memory pressure; fixing it requires both JVM tuning and application-level optimization.

🔹 Scenario 2 – Memory Leak

Problem:

Service crashes after hours with OutOfMemoryError.

Heap dump reveals objects not collected by GC.

Why it happens:

Objects are unintentionally retained, e.g., in static collections, caches, or session objects.

JVM cannot reclaim memory, leading to crash.

Approach:

Capture heap dump → Snapshot of memory at crash time.

Identify leak suspects → Look for objects with unusually high retention.

Fix code → Properly handle sessions, release static references, or improve cache eviction.

Monitor memory usage post-fix → Ensure leak is resolved.

Takeaway:

Memory leaks are application-level issues, visible after analyzing heap dumps.

Fixing them prevents crashes and stabilizes service.

🔹 Scenario 3 – Slow Database Queries

Problem:

Database queries take 5+ seconds.

Threads are waiting on DB connections.

Overall throughput drops.

Why it happens:

Inefficient SQL queries or missing indexes.

Database connections are exhausted due to slow queries.

Lack of caching or batch processing.

Approach:

Enable SQL logging → Identify slow queries.

Optimize queries & indexes → Make SQL efficient.

Tune connection pool → Increase available connections or adjust timeout.

Add caching → Reduce repeated queries for same data.

Use batch operations → Reduce per-row query overhead.

Takeaway:

Database inefficiency can block threads and degrade app performance.

Combining query optimization + connection tuning + caching usually resolves it.

🔹 Scenario 4 – Thread Deadlock

Problem:

Application hangs.

Thread dump shows threads in BLOCKED state due to synchronized locks.

Why it happens:

Two or more threads are waiting for each other’s locks → circular waiting.

Application cannot proceed because locks are held indefinitely.

Approach:

Analyze thread dump → Identify which threads are deadlocked.

Reduce lock scope → Avoid holding locks longer than needed.

Use Lock API with timeout → Prevent threads from waiting forever.

Adopt concurrent utilities → Use ConcurrentHashMap, Semaphore, or ExecutorService instead of manual locks.

Test under load → Verify that deadlocks no longer occur.

Takeaway:

Deadlocks are synchronization issues that block progress.

Reducing lock contention and using modern concurrency utilities solves the problem


