Top Frequently Asked Interview Questions on Saga Pattern (Microservices)


If you’re preparing for a Microservices interview, you’ll often be asked about how to manage distributed transactions — that’s where the Saga Pattern comes in 👇

Q1. What is the Saga Pattern?
A. It’s a design pattern used to maintain data consistency across multiple microservices without using distributed transactions.

Q2. Why do we need the Saga Pattern?
A. Because in microservices, each service has its own database — so a single transaction spanning multiple databases can’t be handled using traditional ACID transactions.

Q3. What are the types of Sagas?
A. 1) Choreography: Each service publishes and listens to domain events.
2) Orchestration: A central controller (Saga orchestrator) coordinates the sequence of local transactions.

Q4. What are the advantages of using Saga Pattern?
✅ Ensures eventual consistency
✅ Decoupled and scalable
✅ Fault-tolerant with compensating transactions

Q5. What are compensating transactions?
A. They undo the changes made by a previous local transaction when something fails midway.

Q6. What technologies or tools can implement Saga?
A. Kafka, RabbitMQ, Axon Framework, Eventuate Tram, or Spring Boot + Orchestration Service.

Q7. How do you handle failure or retries in Sagas?
A. Use retry strategies, dead-letter queues, and compensating actions to maintain consistency.


